hange:  ```text Error: RuntimeError: infinity is not a finite number ```  The new test `edge_mean_of_large_finite_values_stays_finite` failed with that exact message before the implementation changed.  A later review found that dividing each element by the count could still overflow at the top of the `f64` range, and that `matrix.sum` had the same intermediate-overflow class. Both reductions now fall back to an exact power-of-two scaling before compensated summation, and tests pin the mean and sum at `f64::MAX` with a non-power-of-two count.  ## What changed  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/matrix.rs | +488 | dense row-major `f64` matrix, argument validation, shape checks, elementwise operations, `ndarray` matmul/transpose/reductions, indexing and display | | src/matrix.rs | +41 −5 | `matrix.sum` and `matrix.mean` use compensated summation, scale by an exact power of two when that sum would overflow, divide the mean after restoring the scale, check `from_lists` for the element limit before copying, and name the bound for a fractional index | | src/lib.rs | +1 | registers the `matrix` module | | src/runtime.rs | +6 | dispatches `matrix.*` through the one builtin path and returns a row for `matrix[row]` | | src/value.rs | +11 −4 | matrix display and `type_of` name | | src/stdlib.rs | +154 | module, twenty members, builtin names, signatures and the registry loop | | Cargo.toml | +1 | `ndarray = "0.15"` | | Cargo.lock | +48 | the `ndarray` dependency graph | | SPEC.md | +65 | matrix surface, `f64` promotion, exact-shape rules, empty dimensions, indexing and binding cost | | SPEC.md | +3 −1 | documents compensated summation and the power-of-two overflow fallback for `matrix.sum` and `matrix.mean` | | README.md | +33 | the same public surface in the README | | SURVEY.md | +59 −58 | re-anchors registry counts and citations; records the `matrix` module | | tests/matrix_test.rs | +390 | sixteen matrix tests (five added by this run) | | tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs | +52 | three tracked phase-106 tests, including failure and out-of-bounds checks | | tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs | +100 | twenty answerable `matrix.*` rows | | tests/stdlib_module_names_test.rs | +5 | a documented `matrix` module call | | tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt | +2 −2 | SPEC line-key re-anchoring | | tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs | +1 | the phase-106 delivery subject is acknowledged by the delivery rule, not by a stale SHA |  The value is `Value::Object("matrix", …)` with `rows`, `cols` and a flat row-major `data` field, so no variant was added to the public `Value` enum. All elements are Redblue numbers (`f64`); integer-looking literals promote without truncation. Elementwise operations require identical shapes and never broadcast; a mismatch names both shapes. Matmul names both operand shapes when the inner dimensions disagree. Division by zero, non-finite results, empty `mean`/`min`/`max`, negative or fractional indexes, and matrices over 1,000,000 elements are catchable `RuntimeError`s. Empty `sum` is `0`; `0x0`, `Nx0` and `0xN` shapes have defined elementwise, transpose and reduction behaviour.  The twenty members are `zeros`, `ones`, `identity`, `from_lists`, `to_lists`, `shape`, `add`, `subtract`, `multiply`, `divide`, `scale`, `matmul`, `transpose`, `sum`, `mean`, `min`, `max`, `at`, `row` and `column`.  ## Cost  The same 80×80 multiplication was measured on this host with the debug binary, including the `./target/debug/rb` process start:  | Form | Runs | Mean wall clock | |---|---|---| | `matrix.matmul` over `matrix.ones(80, 80)` | 3 | 6.0 ms | | Three nested Redblue loops over nested lists | 3 | 1394.7 ms |  The binding is about 232× faster per run; the nested-list result is dominated by interpreted loop and list indexing, not by matrix storage. The nested-list version transposed `b` with `push`, then used indexed `for each` loops for the product.  ## Tests added  | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | matrix_multiply_matches_hand_written_numbers | numeric boundary / exact arithmetic | | elementwise_operations_and_reductions_assert_literal_values | nesting / all elementwise and reduction paths | | edge_zero_by_zero_matrix_has_defined_behaviour | empty | | edge_shape_mismatch_is_refused_and_names_both_shapes | type mismatch / malformed input | | edge_integer_and_float_promotion_does_not_truncate | numeric boundary | | edge_large_values_lose_f64_precision_and_overflow_is_refused | numeric boundary / precision | | edge_out_of_bounds_and_type_mismatch_are_caught_errors | out_of_bounds / type mismatch | | edge_indexing_returns_rows_and_single_elements | boundary / indexing | | edge_dimension_limit_and_bad_dimensions_are_refused | resource limit / malformed input | | edge_both_engines_produce_equal_matrix_data | cross-engine equality | | edge_mean_of_large_finite_values_stays_finite | numeric boundary / large finite values including `f64::MAX` with a non-power-of-two count (new) | | edge_mean_sums_before_dividing_for_exact_and_tiny_values | numeric boundary / exact, odd-count and subnormal means (new) | | edge_non_square_multiply_matches_hand_written_numbers | boundary / non-square multiply (new) | | edge_rank_mismatch_is_refused_by_name | nesting / rank mismatch, asserts a failure (new) | | edge_sum_keeps_large_f64_magnitudes_past_i64 | numeric boundary / f64 magnitude past i64 and finite sums with overflowing intermediates (new) | | edge_divide_refuses_a_zero_divisor_but_divides_by_non_zero | numeric boundary / asserts a failure | | matrix_matmul_shape_and_reductions_are_exact | numeric boundary / exact arithmetic | | edge_phase106_matrix_shape_mismatch_is_a_clean_error | type mismatch / asserts a failure | | edge_phase106_matrix_out_of_bounds_is_a_clean_error | out_of_bounds / asserts a failure |  `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` also recognises `resume phase-106:` as this phase's delivery subject, so the adopted work commit is acknowledged by the existing rule rather than pinned to a SHA the pipeline rebuilds. The doc-block line keys moved by two with the SPEC paragraph.  ## Edge-case matrix  | Class | Status | |---|---| | empty | covered — `0x0`, `Nx0`, `0xN`; empty `sum`; empty `mean`/`min`/`max` refuse | | singleton | covered — 1×1 construction, elementwise, transpose, indexing and mean | | boundary | covered — row indexing, `at`, transpose of a vector-shaped matrix, non-square matmul | | out_of_bounds | covered — negative, fractional and past-end indexes refuse | | type_mismatch | covered — non-number elements, non-matrix operands and shape mismatch refuse by name | | numeric_boundary | covered — integer promotion, exact f64 precision loss, exact and subnormal means, finite means and sums at `f64::MAX`, f64 magnitude past i64 and overflow refusal | | unicode | N/A — the surface accepts numbers only | | nesting_recursion | covered — `from_lists` validates nested row depth; a flat or rank-3 row is refused, and deep recursion is not exposed | | duplicate_missing_keys | N/A — matrices have positional dimensions, not keys | | malformed_input | covered — ragged rows, bad dimensions and empty reductions refuse | | resource_limit | covered — the 1,000,000-element ceiling and dimension validation |  ## Gates  | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test --all-targets | pass — 2592 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored | | rbops/verify.sh phase-106 | pass |  ## Invariants touched  - Dotted calls remain gated by `module_has_member`; `matrix` and its twenty   members are in `MODULES` and `MODULE_MEMBERS` in both directions. - The public `Value` enum is unchanged; matrix is an object with private field   names, and existing display/`type_of` behaviour is extended by name. - Both engines reach the same matrix implementation through `runtime::builtin`;   a test asserts equal data from the tree interpreter and the bytecode VM. - Matrix operations do not mutate their operands; every result is a fresh   matrix, and `to_lists` returns fresh nested lists. - Errors are catchable and name the offending shape, index, dimension or type.  ## Known gaps / follow-ups  - No broadcasting, complex numbers, matrix slicing, linear solves or   decompositions; the contract is exact-shape arithmetic, matmul and reductions. - `from_lists` accepts only rectangular nested lists of numbers and refuses   ragged input by row. - Storage is row-major `f64`; very large values follow `f64` precision and   non-finite results are refused rather than stored.  ```
