   6030       33:12 opencode run --model opencode/deepseek-v4.1-flash-free --dir /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue --agent build --title rbops-phase-106 # DO THIS, NOW. No orientation phase.  Your working directory is the project checkout. It is the ONLY place you may read or write. The RBOPS pipeline that invoked you lives elsewhere; do not inspect it, do not audit it, and do not spend a single tool call on it.  You have one job, described below. Work in this order and do not deviate:  1. Reproduce the finding. Smallest command that shows it. Then STOP and note it. 2. Write ONE failing test that demonstrates it. Run ONLY your targeted test: `cargo test <test_name>` (fast). Do NOT run `cargo test --all-targets` during iteration — the full suite runs 67 binaries, takes 15 minutes, and will burn your time budget. Watch your test fail. 3. Make the smallest change that turns that test green. Re-run `cargo test <test_name>`. 4. Add the remaining edge-case tests required below. Test each with `cargo test <test_name>`. 5. Only now run the four gates (full suite at the end):    - cargo fmt --all -- --check    - cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    - cargo test --all-targets    - ../rbops/verify.sh "phase-106" 6. Write REPORT.md.  Budget: reproduce in 2 minutes, first test by 5, first edit by 10. If you still have not written code by then, you have misread the task — re-read this message. Do not browse. Do not read files you have not been told to read. Do not write a plan document. There is no human to review a plan.  Files: read and write ONLY inside your working directory. Never write to /tmp, /home, or anywhere outside the project — the permission system auto-rejects those calls, and in this pipeline a rejected tool call ends your run. If you need scratch space, use ./target/tmp/ inside the project.  When you are done, say DONE and stop.  ---  ## Contract you must obey (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md — RBOPS (Redblue Autonomous Ops)  This file is the contract for every agent that RBOPS dispatches. It is injected verbatim into every phase context by `rbops/dispatch.sh`. It is law, not advice.  You are running inside an automated pipeline. There is no human in your loop. The only thing that makes your work count is that it passes a machine gate. Nothing you *say* about your work is evidence. Only what `rbops/verify.sh` proves is evidence.  ---  ## 0. What RBOPS is  RBOPS is a self-dispatching, self-auditing development pipeline for the **Redblue** programming language.  - **Dispatcher** (`rbops/dispatch.sh`) — picks the next eligible phase from   `rbops/phases.json`, runs it, records state, gates it, triggers the next tick. - **Manifest** (`rbops/phases.json`) — the single source of truth. Machine-readable.   If a phase is not in the manifest, it does not exist. - **Gate** (`rbops/verify.sh`) — the only authority on whether work is real. - **Auditor** (`.opencode/agent/auditor.md`) — periodically reads the codebase,   finds new defects and missing capabilities, and **writes new phases**. - **Bootstrap** — the endgame: Redblue compiles Redblue.  ### The state machine  ```   IDLE ──dispatch──▶ RUNNING ──verify ok──▶ REVIEW ──ok──▶ .done ──▶ next phase                       │                       ├─ verify fail ──▶ RETRY (attempt+1)                       ├─ model error ──▶ .deferred (retry later tick)                       ├─ max attempts ──▶ .blocked (needs human)                       └─ budget spent ──▶ .starved (needs new phases) ```  State is **git**, not a database. Each phase directory in `phases/` carries marker files that are committed. Stateless runners therefore work.  ---  ## 1. HARD RULES — violating any of these fails the phase  1. **Do not touch RBOPS itself.** Never edit `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`,    `rbops/phases.json`, or any `.opencode/agent/*.md`. The bot token has no    `workflows` scope and a push touching `.github/` is refused. Do not waste a    phase discovering this. 2. **Never weaken a gate.** You may not modify `verify.sh`, delete a failing    test, add `#[ignore]`, add `.skip`, loosen an assertion, lower a threshold,    raise a timeout to dodge a hang, or comment out a test body. **Any of these    is an automatic phase failure and a review-blocking finding.** There is no    exception for "it was flaky". Flaky tests get fixed, not muted. 3. **No claiming success you did not achieve.** If `verify.sh` is red, the phase    is not done. Report the failure honestly in `REPORT.md` and exit. 4. **Do not run long builds locally.** CI is the only place `cargo build` runs.    Locally use `cargo check --all-targets` at most, and only if essential. 5. **Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens.** Check `.gitignore` covers    `.env`, `*.key`, `target/`. 6. **No destructive git.** No `push --force` to `main`, no `reset --hard` on    shared history, no rewriting other phases' commits. 7. **One phase, one concern.** If you discover work that does not belong to your    phase, do not do it. Record it in `phases/phase-NNN/FINDINGS.md` so the    auditor can promote it to a real phase. 8. **No unbounded speculation.** Do not rewrite the architecture, rename public    types, change the `.rb` file extension, or redesign the grammar without an    explicit phase that says so.  ---  ## 2. Redblue — what must never silently change  Redblue is a tree-walking interpreter: `Lexer → Parser → Analyzer → VM`. Its contract is *readable English-like source*.  **Language invariants.** A phase may not change these without explicitly re-opening the language design in its own PROMPT:  | Invariant | Why | |---|---| | `.rb` is the source extension | Tooling, `rb run`, LSP | | `to … end`, `if … end`, `for … end` use `end`, never braces | Core philosophy | | `set x to <expr>` is assignment | Grammar | | `say` prints | 100% of examples depend on it | | `Value` variants are `Nothing/Number/Text/YesNo/List/Record/Object/Function/Builtin` | Public API, `redblue::Value` is exported | | Errors are `Error::{Lexer,Parser,Analyzer,Runtime,Io}` | Public API | | Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax | Parser tests | | Existing tests in `tests/` keep passing | — |  **Backwards compatibility.** `examples/*.rb` and `modules/*.rb` are the language's specification-by-example. Every phase must keep them working. The gate runs them.  ---  ## 3. Testing mandate — this is where most phases fail  > Every test must assert something that can fail.  Redblue's current test layer is broken in exactly this way, which is why Phase 1 exists. From the moment you touch behaviour, your code is held to the standard the rest of the project has not yet reached.  ### 3.1 Every test must have  1. **A real assertion.** A Redblue test must use `expect <expr> to be <value>`,    `expect <expr> to contain <value>`, or `assert.equal`. If your test only    "runs without crashing", it is a smoke test: label it `// smoke` and it will    not count toward the phase's test quota. 2. **At least one edge case.** The happy path alone is not a test. 3. **A named failure message.** `expect x to be 5` must be able to fail with a    useful message. 4. **Determinism.** No wall-clock, no filesystem outside a temp dir, no network,    no reliance on `HashMap` iteration order.  ### 3.2 Mandatory edge-case matrix  For **every** behaviour you implement or change, test at minimum:  - **Empty / zero / "nothing"** — empty list, empty text, `nothing`, `0`, `""` - **Singleton and boundary** — exactly one element; index `0` and index `len-1` - **Out of bounds** — index `-1`, index `len`, index `999` (must be a clean   runtime error, never a panic, never UB) - **Type mismatch** — number where text expected, record where list expected - **Type coercion boundaries** — `0/0`, `1/0`, `-0.0`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`,   `2^53±1` (precision), `-2^31`, integer overflow past `i64` - **Unicode and escapes** — empty text, `"`, `\`, newline in string,   emoji/CJK/RTL, combining marks, very long strings - **Nesting and recursion** — nested lists, nested records, 3+ closure/object   scopes, mutual recursion - **Duplicate and missing keys** — record with repeated keys, missing field access - **Malformed input** — unterminated string, unclosed `end`, stray token,   empty file, BOM, CRLF line endings, non-UTF8 bytes - **Resource and state** — file that does not exist, permission denied, path with   spaces, deeply nested call stack, infinite loop guard  Pick the rows that apply. A phase that changes the VM must justify in `REPORT.md` which rows are N/A and why.  ### 3.3 Test quota (hard floor, not a target)  The gate enforces a floor: **≥ 3 new `#[test]` functions or ≥ 2 new Redblue `test` blocks**, plus **≥ 1 test named `edge_*`**, **≥ 1 test that asserts a failure is produced**, and **zero** newly-skipped or newly-ignored tests.  Both mandatory tests must be expressible in whichever language you are working in. Write them like this:  | Requirement | Rust | Redblue | |---|---|---| | edge case test | `fn edge_empty_list() { … }` | `test "edge_empty list is rejected" do` | | asserts a failure | `assert!(r.is_err())` / `#[should_panic]` | `try … catch error … end` |  A Redblue test name is a **string literal**, so it is `test "edge_foo"`, with a quote after `test `. Likewise Redblue has no exceptions: `try … catch error` is its failure-catching construct, and a test that faults without one is not asserting anything. Both forms are recognised by the gate.  **Not every phase has a failure channel.** A linter reports problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP emit documents. There is no `Result::Err` to assert on, so the "asserts a failure is produced" rule cannot apply to them. Phases 015, 016 and 017 declare `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the gate **skips that one check and says so**. Every other phase keeps it at full strength — a phase that changes what the interpreter *executes* must prove it produces a clean error when it should.  Deliberately, the count floor is low. It exists to catch "no verification at all", not to judge sufficiency — a hard 6 once failed a genuinely good phase with 5 edge tests, which is the gate being smarter than the reviewer. Judging whether the tests COVER the change is the reviewer's job (AGENTS.md section 5: an untested branch is a MAJOR finding, fixed and re-gated). The gate is the floor — cheap, mechanical, never wrong in the pass direction. The reviewer is the ceiling.  ### 3.5 You must implement, not only test  Most rules in this file measure *verification*. None of them measure *ambition*, so the cheapest way to pass the gate would be to write tests for behaviour that already exists and change no implementation at all. That is not a phase.  Every phase in `rbops/phases.json` from 011 onward declares `must_touch` — the areas where that phase's work must land. The gate fails the phase if **no** changed file falls under any of them:  ```json "must_touch": ["src/"]                 // phases 011-013, 015, 016, 018, 019 "must_touch": ["src/", "modules/"]// phase 014, 024 — either is acceptable "must_touch": ["src/", "bootstrap/"]    // phases 021-023 "must_touch": ["tests/"]                // phase 020 — a harness, test-only by design ```  Read your own phase's entry before you start. If your finding genuinely cannot be fixed in the declared area, that is a manifest bug: say so in `FINDINGS.md` with the file:line, and fix it in the area the finding names rather than bending the phase. Phase-024 is the worked example — its finding cites both `src/lexer.rs` and `modules/MathUtils.rb`, so both are declared and either is a valid answer.  Note what this rule is **not**: it is a floor, satisfied by one changed line under `src/`. It says "you implemented something", not "you implemented enough". Judging that the change is correct, complete and proportionate is the reviewer's job, and an `edge_*` test that does not actually pin the new behaviour is a BLOCKER finding.  ### 3.4 The four gates, in order  ``` cargo fmt --check          # formatting        (fails on any diff) cargo clippy -- -D warnings# lints, zero warnings tolerated cargo test                 # full suite, 0 failures ./rbops/verify.sh          # project-specific gates ```  ---  ## 4. REPORT.md contract — required, machine-checked  `phases/phase-NNN/REPORT.md` must exist. `verify.sh` rejects the phase without it.  ```markdown # Phase NNN — <title>  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/vm.rs | +40 −12 | call depth counter in call_builtin |  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | edge_call_stack_overflow | out of bounds / resource | | test_zero_division | type/numeric boundary |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test | 42 passed, 0 failed | | rbops/verify.sh | pass |  ## Invariants touched - None  /  - `expect` now returns a catchable Runtime error (was: process abort)  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - No stack-depth limit for mutually recursive objects yet → FINDINGS.md ```  A REPORT.md with empty tables, or with a `Gates` table that does not match what `verify.sh` printed, is a **fake completion** and will be caught by the auditor.  ---  ## 5. Reviewer contract  The reviewer (`.opencode/agent/reviewer.md`) is **read-only**: no edit, no bash. It reviews the phase diff after the gate is green and emits `FINDINGS:`. Findings are then fixed by a fresh implementer run, and **the full gate is run again**. A phase with open BLOCKER/MAJOR findings is not `.done`.  The reviewer must specifically hunt for:  - **Fake completion** — REPORT.md claims a gate passed that it did not; tests   that cannot fail; assertions weakened; thresholds lowered. - **Panic paths** — any reachable `panic!`/`unwrap`/indexing/`unreachable!`. - **Unbounded resource use** — no recursion limit, no loop guard, no output cap. - **Nondeterminism** — `HashMap` iteration reaching output, floats in equality. - **Error-message regressions** — a previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-106  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-106 — Add matrix and tensor values bound to Rust crates, the numpy way  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `major` | | Risk | `high` | | Depends on | phase-095 | | Timeout | 240 min | | Must change | `src/` |  ## You must change `src/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [src/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` There is no numeric container at all, so numeric work means nested lists and nested loops in Redblue code, which is both unreadable and slow enough to be unusable. This is the phase that answers 'can Redblue have PyTorch' honestly: it cannot, written in Redblue, because tensor and autograd work is fast only when native - that is exactly why numpy and PyTorch are C/C++ and not Python. An interpreted Redblue is slower than Python, so the answer is a binding, the same answer Python reached. Redblue already binds crates this way: network.get wraps reqwest and files.read wraps std::fs. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Add a numeric array value backed by a Rust linear-algebra crate, with elementwise operations, matrix multiply, reductions, shape, and indexing, all as bindings rather than as Redblue implementations. Follow the binding policy in LIBRARIES.md. Document the type promotion rules explicitly, because a multiply that silently truncates an integer is the kind of defect that is invisible until it is expensive. Scope this phase to a dense array and its arithmetic; autograd, GPU and anything else is a separate decision that should not be smuggled in here.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-106    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] a Redblue program builds an array, multiplies two matrices, and asserts the result against literal expected numbers written out by hand - [ ] elementwise operations and reductions assert literal values on a known input, not values recomputed by the same code path - [ ] shape mismatches are refused with a catchable error naming both shapes, and no operation silently broadcasts - [ ] integer and floating operations follow a documented promotion rule, and a test asserts the documented rule on a mixed-type case - [ ] an empty array and a zero-dimension operation are defined and asserted rather than undefined behaviour - [ ] the tree-walker and the bytecode VM produce equal results, compared as data across both engines - [ ] SPEC.md documents the promotion rules and the operations, and states plainly that this is a binding to a crate and not Redblue-native - [ ] REPORT.md records the measured cost of the bound operations against the equivalent nested-list Redblue code, so the binding's value is evidenced - [ ] edge_* tests cover a 0x0 matrix, a rank mismatch, a non-square multiply, a very large value that loses precision in f64, and integer overflow in a sum  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-106/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 106 — Matrix and numeric array binding  ## Finding reproduced  ```bash printf 'say matrix.multiply(matrix.ones(2, 2), matrix.ones(2, 2))\n' > target/tmp_repro.rb ./target/debug/rb run target/tmp_repro.rb ```  Before the binding existed:  ```text Error: AnalyzerError: Unknown variable 'matrix' ```  The adopted work in `HEAD` added the binding. This resumed run re-verified it and found one remaining arithmetic defect: `matrix.mean` summed first, so two finite `1e308` values overflowed to infinity and the finite mean of `1e308` was refused.  ```bash printf 'say matrix.mean(matrix.from_lists([[1e308, 1e308]]))\n' > target/tmp/repro_mean.rb ./target/debug/rb run target/tmp/repro_mean.rb ```  Before this run's change:  ```text Error: RuntimeError: infinity is not a finite number ```  The new test `edge_mean_of_large_finite_values_stays_finite` failed with that exact message before the implementation changed.  A later review found that dividing each element by the count could still overflow at the top of the `f64` range, and that `matrix.sum` had the same intermediate-overflow class. Both reductions now fall back to an exact power-of-two scaling before compensated summation, and tests pin the mean and sum at `f64::MAX` with a non-power-of-two count.  ## What changed  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/matrix.rs | +488 | dense row-major `f64` matrix, argument validation, shape checks, elementwise operations, `ndarray` matmul/transpose/reductions, indexing and display | | src/matrix.rs | +41 −5 | `matrix.sum` and `matrix.mean` use compensated summation, scale by an exact power of two when that sum would overflow, divide the mean after restoring the scale, check `from_lists` for the element limit before copying, and name the bound for a fractional index | | src/lib.rs | +1 | registers the `matrix` module | | src/runtime.rs | +6 | dispatches `matrix.*` through the one builtin path and returns a row for `matrix[row]` | | src/value.rs | +11 −4 | matrix display and `type_of` name | | src/stdlib.rs | +154 | module, twenty members, builtin names, signatures and the registry loop | | Cargo.toml | +1 | `ndarray = "0.15"` | | Cargo.lock | +48 | the `ndarray` dependency graph | | SPEC.md | +65 | matrix surface, `f64` promotion, exact-shape rules, empty dimensions, indexing and binding cost | | SPEC.md | +3 −1 | documents compensated summation and the power-of-two overflow fallback for `matrix.sum` and `matrix.mean` | | README.md | +33 | the same public surface in the README | | SURVEY.md | +59 −58 | re-anchors registry counts and citations; records the `matrix` module | | tests/matrix_test.rs | +390 | sixteen matrix tests (five added by this run) | | tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs | +52 | three tracked phase-106 tests, including failure and out-of-bounds checks | | tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs | +100 | twenty answerable `matrix.*` rows | | tests/stdlib_module_names_test.rs | +5 | a documented `matrix` module call | | tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt | +2 −2 | SPEC line-key re-anchoring | | tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs | +1 | the phase-106 delivery subject is acknowledged by the delivery rule, not by a stale SHA |  The value is `Value::Object("matrix", …)` with `rows`, `cols` and a flat row-major `data` field, so no variant was added to the public `Value` enum. All elements are Redblue numbers (`f64`); integer-looking literals promote without truncation. Elementwise operations require identical shapes and never broadcast; a mismatch names both shapes. Matmul names both operand shapes when the inner dimensions disagree. Division by zero, non-finite results, empty `mean`/`min`/`max`, negative or fractional indexes, and matrices over 1,000,000 elements are catchable `RuntimeError`s. Empty `sum` is `0`; `0x0`, `Nx0` and `0xN` shapes have defined elementwise, transpose and reduction behaviour.  The twenty members are `zeros`, `ones`, `identity`, `from_lists`, `to_lists`, `shape`, `add`, `subtract`, `multiply`, `divide`, `scale`, `matmul`, `transpose`, `sum`, `mean`, `min`, `max`, `at`, `row` and `column`.  ## Cost  The same 80×80 multiplication was measured on this host with the debug binary, including the `./target/debug/rb` process start:  | Form | Runs | Mean wall clock | |---|---|---| | `matrix.matmul` over `matrix.ones(80, 80)` | 3 | 6.0 ms | | Three nested Redblue loops over nested lists | 3 | 1394.7 ms |  The binding is about 232× faster per run; the nested-list result is dominated by interpreted loop and list indexing, not by matrix storage. The nested-list version transposed `b` with `push`, then used indexed `for each` loops for the product.  ## Tests added  | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | matrix_multiply_matches_hand_written_numbers | numeric boundary / exact arithmetic | | elementwise_operations_and_reductions_assert_literal_values | nesting / all elementwise and reduction paths | | edge_zero_by_zero_matrix_has_defined_behaviour | empty | | edge_shape_mismatch_is_refused_and_names_both_shapes | type mismatch / malformed input | | edge_integer_and_float_promotion_does_not_truncate | numeric boundary | | edge_large_values_lose_f64_precision_and_overflow_is_refused | numeric boundary / precision | | edge_out_of_bounds_and_type_mismatch_are_caught_errors | out_of_bounds / type mismatch | | edge_indexing_returns_rows_and_single_elements | boundary / indexing | | edge_dimension_limit_and_bad_dimensions_are_refused | resource limit / malformed input | | edge_both_engines_produce_equal_matrix_data | cross-engine equality | | edge_mean_of_large_finite_values_stays_finite | numeric boundary / large finite values including `f64::MAX` with a non-power-of-two count (new) | | edge_mean_sums_before_dividing_for_exact_and_tiny_values | numeric boundary / exact, odd-count and subnormal means (new) | | edge_non_square_multiply_matches_hand_written_numbers | boundary / non-square multiply (new) | | edge_rank_mismatch_is_refused_by_name | nesting / rank mismatch, asserts a failure (new) | | edge_sum_keeps_large_f64_magnitudes_past_i64 | numeric boundary / f64 magnitude past i64 and finite sums with overflowing intermediates (new) | | edge_divide_refuses_a_zero_divisor_but_divides_by_non_zero | numeric boundary / asserts a failure | | matrix_matmul_shape_and_reductions_are_exact | numeric boundary / exact arithmetic | | edge_phase106_matrix_shape_mismatch_is_a_clean_error | type mismatch / asserts a failure | | edge_phase106_matrix_out_of_bounds_is_a_clean_error | out_of_bounds / asserts a failure |  `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` also recognises `resume phase-106:` as this phase's delivery subject, so the adopted work commit is acknowledged by the existing rule rather than pinned to a SHA the pipeline rebuilds. The doc-block line keys moved by two with the SPEC paragraph.  ## Edge-case matrix  | Class | Status | |---|---| | empty | covered — `0x0`, `Nx0`, `0xN`; empty `sum`; empty `mean`/`min`/`max` refuse | | singleton | covered — 1×1 construction, elementwise, transpose, indexing and mean | | boundary | covered — row indexing, `at`, transpose of a vector-shaped matrix, non-square matmul | | out_of_bounds | covered — negative, fractional and past-end indexes refuse | | type_mismatch | covered — non-number elements, non-matrix operands and shape mismatch refuse by name | | numeric_boundary | covered — integer promotion, exact f64 precision loss, exact and subnormal means, finite means and sums at `f64::MAX`, f64 magnitude past i64 and overflow refusal | | unicode | N/A — the surface accepts numbers only | | nesting_recursion | covered — `from_lists` validates nested row depth; a flat or rank-3 row is refused, and deep recursion is not exposed | | duplicate_missing_keys | N/A — matrices have positional dimensions, not keys | | malformed_input | covered — ragged rows, bad dimensions and empty reductions refuse | | resource_limit | covered — the 1,000,000-element ceiling and dimension validation |  ## Gates  | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test --all-targets | pass — 2592 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored | | rbops/verify.sh phase-106 | pass |  ## Invariants touched  - Dotted calls remain gated by `module_has_member`; `matrix` and its twenty   members are in `MODULES` and `MODULE_MEMBERS` in both directions. - The public `Value` enum is unchanged; matrix is an object with private field   names, and existing display/`type_of` behaviour is extended by name. - Both engines reach the same matrix implementation through `runtime::builtin`;   a test asserts equal data from the tree interpreter and the bytecode VM. - Matrix operations do not mutate their operands; every result is a fresh   matrix, and `to_lists` returns fresh nested lists. - Errors are catchable and name the offending shape, index, dimension or type.  ## Known gaps / follow-ups  - No broadcasting, complex numbers, matrix slicing, linear solves or   decompositions; the contract is exact-shape arithmetic, matmul and reductions. - `from_lists` accepts only rectangular nested lists of numbers and refuses   ragged input by row. - Storage is row-major `f64`; very large values follow `f64` precision and   non-finite results are refused rather than stored.  ```
