 Error: RuntimeError: infinity is not a finite number ```  The new test `edge_mean_of_large_finite_values_stays_finite` failed with that exact message before the implementation changed.  A later review found that dividing each element by the count could still overflow at the top of the `f64` range, and that `matrix.sum` had the same intermediate-overflow class. Both reductions now fall back to an exact power-of-two scaling before compensated summation, and tests pin the mean and sum at `f64::MAX` with a non-power-of-two count.  ## What changed  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/matrix.rs | +488 | dense row-major `f64` matrix, argument validation, shape checks, elementwise operations, `ndarray` matmul/transpose/reductions, indexing and display | | src/matrix.rs | +41 −5 | `matrix.sum` and `matrix.mean` use compensated summation, scale by an exact power of two when that sum would overflow, divide the mean after restoring the scale, check `from_lists` for the element limit before copying, and name the bound for a fractional index | | src/lib.rs | +1 | registers the `matrix` module | | src/runtime.rs | +6 | dispatches `matrix.*` through the one builtin path and returns a row for `matrix[row]` | | src/value.rs | +11 −4 | matrix display and `type_of` name | | src/stdlib.rs | +154 | module, twenty members, builtin names, signatures and the registry loop | | Cargo.toml | +1 | `ndarray = "0.15"` | | Cargo.lock | +48 | the `ndarray` dependency graph | | SPEC.md | +65 | matrix surface, `f64` promotion, exact-shape rules, empty dimensions, indexing and binding cost | | SPEC.md | +3 −1 | documents compensated summation and the power-of-two overflow fallback for `matrix.sum` and `matrix.mean` | | README.md | +33 | the same public surface in the README | | SURVEY.md | +59 −58 | re-anchors registry counts and citations; records the `matrix` module | | tests/matrix_test.rs | +390 | sixteen matrix tests (five added by this run) | | tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs | +52 | three